<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Uaxactun</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 900 CE (approx.) · capital Uaxactun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya city in the Petén just north of Tikal, home to one of the first excavated Maya astronomical complexes and long overshadowed by its larger neighbour.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Uaxactun was settled in the Middle Preclassic and developed monumental architecture by about 300 BCE. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-preclassic">Late Preclassic</a> pyramid E-VII-sub, decorated with large stucco masks, belongs to an 'E Group', a plaza arrangement once interpreted as aligned to solstice and equinox sunrises.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Uaxactun was ruled by its own lords, but its history is entwined with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tikal">Tikal</a>, about 20 kilometres to the south. Inscriptions record the arrival of the Teotihuacan-linked lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaj-kak">Sihyaj K'ahk'</a> in 378 CE, after which Uaxactun appears under Tikal's influence. Its Early Classic murals and palace sequence were studied in detail by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnegie-institution-for-science">Carnegie Institution</a> in the 1920s and 1930s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Monument building ended around 900 CE, during the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-maya-collapse">Classic Maya collapse</a>, and the city was abandoned. Its excavation produced a ceramic sequence that long served as the basis for dating the Maya lowlands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uaxactun.jpg" alt="Uaxactun" width="61" height="58"><figcaption>Image: Zykasaa at Lithuanian Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uaxactun.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Uaxactun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Siaan Kan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=300&amp;focus=uaxactun" aria-label="Uaxactun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2245 1532 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uaxactun lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2565 1788L2564 1794"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2564 1794h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2565 1788h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2565 1788h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=300&amp;focus=uaxactun">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

